\subsection{Targeted Interventions}
\label{sec:interventions}

The capability trap could stem from prompt design, self-reference circularity, or model-generation limitations. We test three interventions, each targeting a distinct hypothesized cause. Contrastive extraction forces the model to generate answer-discriminative constraints by presenting all candidate answers in the prompt. Cross-model extraction uses Mistral-7B to extract constraints from Qwen2.5-14B traces, breaking the self-reference loop. Generational comparison evaluates Qwen3-14B to test whether next-generation capabilities resolve the trap. All three interventions fail to produce statistically significant improvement over SC (Table~\ref{tab:interventions}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Targeted interventions on FOLIO ($n{=}204$ except $^\dagger$: $n{=}182$, extraction failures excluded). None reaches significance ($\alpha = 0.05$).}
\label{tab:interventions}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Condition & SC (\%) & SICA (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p$ \\
\midrule
Qwen2.5-14B (baseline) & 75.00 & 74.51 & $-$0.49 & n.s. \\
\;\; + Contrastive$^\dagger$ & 73.63 & 72.53 & $-$1.10 & 0.752 \\
\;\; + Cross-model & 75.00 & 75.00 & 0.00 & --- \\
Qwen3-14B (generational) & 83.82 & 84.31 & $+$0.49 & 0.655 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Contrastive Extraction.}
Rather than extracting constraints from individual traces, contrastive extraction presents all candidate answers and explicitly requests constraints that distinguish between them. On the 182 FOLIO problems with successful extraction, contrastive-SICA achieves 72.53\% versus SC 73.63\% ($\Delta = -1.10$\,pp, $p = 0.752$). The discriminative rate is 6.0\%, below the baseline 8.2\%. Despite explicit instructions to generate answer-discriminative constraints, the model produces constraints that remain coupled to its preferred answer: 43.2\% supporting and 56.8\% opposing constraints distribute along the same answer bias present in the original traces.

\paragraph{Generational Comparison: Qwen3-14B.}
Qwen3-14B in non-thinking mode tests whether next-generation model capabilities break the trap. SC improves from 75.00\% (Qwen2.5-14B) to 83.82\%, an $+8.82$\,pp generational gain in base reasoning. The SICA gap shifts from $-0.49$\,pp to $+0.49$\,pp ($p = 0.655$), with only 5 discordant pairs out of 204 (3 SICA wins, 2 SC wins, 97.5\% agreement). Extraction rate improves marginally from 98.5\% to 99.5\%. Generational capability gains lift both SC and SICA equally without producing discriminative constraints.

\paragraph{Cross-Model Extraction.}
Cross-model extraction uses Mistral-7B-Instruct to extract constraints from Qwen2.5-14B traces, removing the self-reference loop where the same model generates and formalizes reasoning. Cross-model SICA produces identical predictions to SC on all 204 problems ($\Delta = 0.00$\,pp), with a discriminative rate of 0\%. The trace structure, not model identity, determines constraint content: a different model extracting from the same traces produces equally non-discriminative constraints.

